::ALG1_U03_PT_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.1
Question:
Which table shows a linear relationship?
{
=A. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 5, 8, 11, 14#The y-values increase by a constant amount of 3.
~B. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 2, 4, 8, 16#This doubles each time, so the change is not constant.
~C. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 1, 3, 6, 10#The changes are 2, 3, and 4, so the relationship is not linear.
~D. x: 0, 1, 2, 3; y: 9, 7, 6, 6#The changes are not constant.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.3
Question:
What is the y-intercept of y = -2x + 6?
{
~A. -2#This is the slope, not the y-intercept.
~B. (6, 0)#This reverses the coordinates of the y-intercept.
=C. (0, 6)#The y-intercept occurs where x = 0.
~D. 2#This changes the sign and uses the slope value.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
What is the slope between (1, 3) and (4, 12)?
{
~A. 9#This is the change in y before dividing by the change in x.
=B. 3#The slope is (12 - 3)/(4 - 1) = 9/3 = 3.
~C. 4#This uses an x-coordinate instead of the rate of change.
~D. 15#This adds y-values instead of finding change per 1 unit of x.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
Write the equation of a line with slope 4 and y-intercept (0, -1).
{
~A. y \= -x + 4#This reverses the slope and y-intercept.
~B. y \= 4x + 1#The slope is correct, but the intercept sign should be negative.
~C. y \= -4x - 1#The y-intercept is correct, but the slope sign is wrong.
=D. y \= 4x - 1#Use y = mx + b with m = 4 and b = -1.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5
Question:
Which slope is perpendicular to a line with slope 2?
{
~A. 2#This would make a parallel line because the slopes are the same.
=B. -1/2#Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals.
~C. 1/2#This is the reciprocal but the sign must change.
~D. -2#This changes the sign but not the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Solve -3x <= 12.
{
=A. x >\= -4#Divide by -3 and reverse the inequality symbol.
~B. x <\= -4#This finds the boundary but forgets to reverse the inequality after dividing by -3.
~C. x <\= 4#This has the wrong boundary sign and wrong direction.
~D. x >\= 4#The direction is right after reversing, but the boundary sign is wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.3
Question:
Which graph description matches y > x + 2?
{
~A. Solid line y \= x + 2, shade above#The shading is correct, but the boundary should be dashed.
~B. Dashed line y \= x + 2, shade below#The line type is correct, but the shading direction is wrong.
=C. Dashed line y \= x + 2, shade above#Greater than uses a dashed boundary and shades above.
~D. Solid line y \= x + 2, shade below#Both the line type and shading direction are wrong.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.4
Question:
Does (2, 5) satisfy y <= 2x + 1?
{
~A. No, because 5 is greater than 1#The right side must be evaluated as 2(2) + 1.
~B. No, because equality is not included#Equality is included with <=.
~C. Yes, because 2 <\= 5 is true#This compares the x-value instead of substituting into the inequality.
=D. Yes, because 5 <\= 5 is true#Substituting gives equality, and <= includes equality.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
A plan costs $12 to start plus $5 per month. Which equation models total cost y after x months?
{
=A. y \= 5x + 12#The rate is 5 per month and the starting cost is 12.
~B. y \= 17x#This treats the sum of the starting cost and rate as one rate.
~C. y \= 12x + 5#This reverses the starting cost and monthly rate.
~D. y \= x + 17#This uses a monthly rate of 1 instead of 5.
}

::ALG1_U03_PT_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.3
Question:
Which inequality has a solid boundary line and is shaded below when y is isolated?
{
~A. y < -2x + 4#This shades below, but the boundary is dashed because equality is not included.
=B. y <\= -2x + 4#Less than or equal to uses a solid boundary and shades below.
~C. y > -2x + 4#This uses a dashed boundary and shades above.
~D. y >\= -2x + 4#This has a solid boundary but shades above.
}

